<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Tudmir</h1><p class="st-meta">713 CE – 779 CE (approx.) · capital Orihuela</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The autonomous Christian principality of Theodemir in Murcia and Alicante, whose 713 surrender treaty is one of the earliest documents of al-Andalus.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodemir-visigoth">Theodemir</a>, a Visigothic noble who governed the south-east, resisted the Muslim invaders and then negotiated. In April 713 he signed a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-aziz-ibn-musa">Abd al-Aziz ibn Musa</a>, keeping control of seven towns, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orihuela">Orihuela</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mula-murcia">Mula</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorca">Lorca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alicante">Alicante</a>, in return for submission and tribute.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The treaty guaranteed the Christians their lives, property and churches as long as they paid an annual tax in coin and in kind, wheat, barley, vinegar, honey and oil, and did not shelter enemies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodemir-visigoth">Theodemir</a> ruled as a client prince, and the region became known in Arabic as Tudmir after him. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athanagild">Athanagild</a> kept the autonomy for a time.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Arab settlers, especially Syrian troops after 743, moved into the region, eroding Christian autonomy. By the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-i">Abd al-Rahman I</a> it was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba</a>, and in 825 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-ii">Abd al-Rahman II</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> as the capital of the province of Tudmir.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tudmir.jpg" alt="Tudmir" width="183" height="183"><figcaption>Image: Roxanna · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teodomiro_de_Orihuela.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>713 CE – 779 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Orihuela</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Theodemir, Athanagild</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Theodemir, Principality of Tudmir</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=746&amp;focus=tudmir" aria-label="Tudmir on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4656 809 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tudmir lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4976 1065L5035 888M4976 1065L4881 1071M4976 1065L4881 1071"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5035 888h0M4881 1071h0M4881 1071h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4976 1065h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4976 1065h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Orihuela. <a href="/atlas?year=746&amp;focus=tudmir">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
